1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,460
In the next twelve minutes we build the UI
for a small game from an empty Unity

2
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:13,892
project: a main menu, a store, two bars
they share, and a purchase popup.

3
00:00:13,892 --> 00:00:21,204
Every screen in its own scene, set up in
the editor, with only a few lines of code.

4
00:00:21,204 --> 00:00:25,786
Follow along, or jump to any step with the
chapters.

5
00:00:28,286 --> 00:00:33,717
Open the Package Manager, press plus, and
choose Add package from git URL.

6
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:37,094
Paste the address from the docs and press
Add.

7
00:00:37,094 --> 00:00:41,571
Unity installs the framework, and
Addressables comes with it.

8
00:00:44,071 --> 00:00:49,903
Every screen will be an Addressable scene,
so open Addressables Groups once and

9
00:00:49,903 --> 00:00:51,380
create its settings.

10
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:57,433
Set the play mode script to Use Asset
Database, so screens load straight from
your

11
00:00:57,433 --> 00:00:59,500
project when you press Play.

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,959
This window is where you'll build all of
your UI.

13
00:01:05,959 --> 00:01:09,191
In the General tab, press Create Config.

14
00:01:09,191 --> 00:01:13,554
The config remembers where your scripts
and scenes go.

15
00:01:13,554 --> 00:01:20,260
Set the two namespaces to your game's:
Coin Shop dot UI dot Elements, and Coin
Shop

16
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:21,714
dot UI dot States.

17
00:01:24,214 --> 00:01:26,868
Below are the Global Canvas Settings.

18
00:01:26,868 --> 00:01:31,816
Every new screen starts with these: a
portrait phone at ten-eighty by

19
00:01:31,816 --> 00:01:34,756
nineteen-twenty, scaling with the screen.

20
00:01:34,756 --> 00:01:38,485
Change them here once, and every new
screen follows.

21
00:01:38,485 --> 00:01:42,214
Existing screens are never changed behind
your back.

22
00:01:44,714 --> 00:01:51,805
Every piece of UI in this framework is an
element: a screen, a bar or a popup.

23
00:01:51,805 --> 00:01:56,896
Each element is its own small scene with
its own script.

24
00:01:56,896 --> 00:01:59,714
Our first one is the main menu.

25
00:02:02,214 --> 00:02:05,214
In the Elements tab, press New Element.

26
00:02:08,214 --> 00:02:10,571
Name it Main Menu UI Element.

27
00:02:10,571 --> 00:02:16,017
Names end in UI Element, so you always
know what you're looking at.

28
00:02:16,017 --> 00:02:22,682
Tick Include Parameters Class: that gives
us a small class for the data we'll pass

29
00:02:22,682 --> 00:02:24,389
to this screen later.

30
00:02:24,389 --> 00:02:30,567
You could also turn a scene you already
have into an element here, but we're

31
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:31,786
starting fresh.

32
00:02:34,286 --> 00:02:39,000
Press Create UI Element, and give Unity a
moment to compile.

33
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:43,126
You get three things.

34
00:02:43,126 --> 00:02:43,977
The script.

35
00:02:43,977 --> 00:02:45,603
The parameters class.

36
00:02:45,603 --> 00:02:52,027
And a scene for this screen, already set
up the way the framework expects, with the

37
00:02:52,027 --> 00:02:53,885
script attached for you.

38
00:02:53,885 --> 00:02:59,071
If a script with this name already exists,
it is never overwritten.

39
00:03:01,571 --> 00:03:05,090
Double-click the element to open its
scene.

40
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:10,571
Every element scene has the same simple
shape, so let's look at it.

41
00:03:13,071 --> 00:03:17,491
At the top, the element's root, with our
script on it.

42
00:03:17,491 --> 00:03:19,209
Under it, one Canvas.

43
00:03:19,209 --> 00:03:22,892
And under the Canvas, one object called
Root.

44
00:03:22,892 --> 00:03:29,357
Root is the important one: it's what moves
when the screen animates in and out.

45
00:03:31,857 --> 00:03:36,404
So there's one rule: everything you build
goes under Root.

46
00:03:36,404 --> 00:03:38,286
Nothing sits next to it.

47
00:03:40,786 --> 00:03:44,958
Right-click Root and add an image for the
background.

48
00:03:44,958 --> 00:03:47,319
Stretch it to fill the screen.

49
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:48,343
Then a title.

50
00:03:48,343 --> 00:03:54,483
Use normal anchors, exactly like any Unity
UI: the canvas scaler takes care of

51
00:03:54,483 --> 00:03:56,214
different phone sizes.

52
00:03:58,714 --> 00:04:02,927
Now a Play button, and a Store button
under it.

53
00:04:02,927 --> 00:04:07,678
We'll make the Store button work later in
the series.

54
00:04:07,678 --> 00:04:12,429
Here it is in the Game view, on a phone-
sized screen.

55
00:04:14,929 --> 00:04:16,786
Everything lives under Root.

56
00:04:16,786 --> 00:04:19,505
That's what keeps the animations working.

57
00:04:19,505 --> 00:04:20,500
Save the scene.

58
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,542
Every element animates in and out on its
own.

59
00:04:26,542 --> 00:04:30,714
Select the main menu and find its
Animations section.

60
00:04:33,214 --> 00:04:34,969
Pick how it appears.

61
00:04:34,969 --> 00:04:39,619
Each choice tells you in one line what it
looks like.

62
00:04:39,619 --> 00:04:44,357
I'll take Scale: it grows in gently while
it fades in.

63
00:04:46,857 --> 00:04:48,353
And how it leaves.

64
00:04:48,353 --> 00:04:48,768
Fade.

65
00:04:48,768 --> 00:04:52,424
The choice is saved straight into the
scene.

66
00:04:52,424 --> 00:04:55,000
There's nothing else to set up.

67
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:03,671
You can watch all ten presets moving on
the Animations page of the docs.

68
00:05:03,671 --> 00:05:09,500
And from code, you can still pick a
different one for a single call.

69
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,317
In a real project, scenes get edited by
lots of people, and sooner or later

70
00:05:18,317 --> 00:05:21,434
something ends up in the wrong place.

71
00:05:21,434 --> 00:05:26,571
Let's break our menu on purpose and watch
the tools catch it.

72
00:05:29,071 --> 00:05:33,671
I'll drag the title out of Root, so it
sits next to it.

73
00:05:33,671 --> 00:05:37,099
Remember the rule: everything under Root.

74
00:05:37,099 --> 00:05:39,357
Save, and refresh the list.

75
00:05:41,857 --> 00:05:45,242
The menu now shows a warning sign:
fixable.

76
00:05:45,242 --> 00:05:49,965
Validate All writes one clear line per
scene to the Console.

77
00:05:49,965 --> 00:05:56,420
And this toggle shows only the scenes that
need attention, which is handy when you

78
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:57,286
have fifty.

79
00:05:59,786 --> 00:06:01,293
Press Fix Structure.

80
00:06:01,293 --> 00:06:06,266
Before it touches anything, it shows you
exactly what will change.

81
00:06:06,266 --> 00:06:12,294
Two objects sit under the Canvas, so it
will put both inside one new full-screen

82
00:06:12,294 --> 00:06:15,761
object called Content Root, in the same
order.

83
00:06:15,761 --> 00:06:20,357
Nothing moves on screen; it all just
animates together again.

84
00:06:22,857 --> 00:06:25,959
Confirm, and the scene is valid again.

85
00:06:25,959 --> 00:06:32,571
Fixes only create and move objects, never
delete anything, and they undo like any

86
00:06:32,571 --> 00:06:33,633
other change.

87
00:06:33,633 --> 00:06:40,490
Here I'd rather put the title back where
it belongs, so I undo and drag it into
Root

88
00:06:40,490 --> 00:06:41,061
myself.

89
00:06:41,061 --> 00:06:46,857
Anything the tool can't fix safely is
listed under What to fix by hand.

90
00:06:49,357 --> 00:06:56,769
Our game needs two bars that sit on top of
every screen: a top bar with the player's

91
00:06:56,769 --> 00:06:59,857
coins, and a nav bar at the bottom.

92
00:06:59,857 --> 00:07:04,357
Two more elements, made exactly like the
main menu.

93
00:07:06,857 --> 00:07:10,087
A strip at the top with the coin count.

94
00:07:10,087 --> 00:07:14,476
A strip at the bottom with a Home and a
Store button.

95
00:07:14,476 --> 00:07:17,209
Everything under Root, as always.

96
00:07:17,209 --> 00:07:24,000
The rest of each bar's screen stays empty,
so the screen underneath shows through.

97
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:32,687
Each element has its own canvas, and the
Canvas section edits it right here.

98
00:07:32,687 --> 00:07:35,617
Sorting order decides what's on top.

99
00:07:35,617 --> 00:07:38,629
Screens stay at zero, bars go to ten.

100
00:07:38,629 --> 00:07:41,071
Later, popups go to a hundred.

101
00:07:43,571 --> 00:07:47,283
Render mode and scaling are here too, per
element.

102
00:07:47,283 --> 00:07:53,000
They start from the global defaults, and
Use Default Settings puts them back.

103
00:07:55,500 --> 00:08:01,750
The Addressables section shows whether
this scene is Addressable, and its group.

104
00:08:01,750 --> 00:08:08,313
You don't have to set it by hand: when we
build the config, every scene a state uses

105
00:08:08,313 --> 00:08:10,500
is made Addressable for you.

106
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,291
One more screen: the store.

107
00:08:15,291 --> 00:08:19,195
Same steps as before, with three items to
buy.

108
00:08:19,195 --> 00:08:21,571
It slides in from the right.

109
00:08:24,071 --> 00:08:25,646
Now the key idea.

110
00:08:25,646 --> 00:08:32,775
A state is what the player sees at one
moment, written as a list of elements.

111
00:08:32,775 --> 00:08:39,071
The main menu state is the menu, plus the
top bar, plus the nav bar.

112
00:08:41,571 --> 00:08:45,768
Type the name next to New state and press
plus.

113
00:08:45,768 --> 00:08:50,232
It also creates a small class we'll use
from code.

114
00:08:50,232 --> 00:08:55,321
Then add its scenes: start typing and pick
from the list.

115
00:08:55,321 --> 00:08:58,714
The menu, the top bar and the nav bar.

116
00:09:01,214 --> 00:09:06,357
Same for the store: the store screen, with
the same two bars.

117
00:09:08,857 --> 00:09:11,687
Notice the bars are in both states.

118
00:09:11,687 --> 00:09:18,399
That's what shared means: when the player
goes from the menu to the store, the bars

119
00:09:18,399 --> 00:09:23,978
stay loaded and on screen, and only the
screen in the middle changes.

120
00:09:23,978 --> 00:09:26,000
No reloading, no flicker.

121
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:32,357
Back in the General tab, press Build
Runtime UiConfig.

122
00:09:34,857 --> 00:09:39,505
It wrote the runtime config: every state
and its scenes.

123
00:09:39,505 --> 00:09:46,476
It made every one of those scenes
Addressable, in a group called UI
Elements, so the

124
00:09:46,476 --> 00:09:52,286
game can load them when it needs them and
unload them when it doesn't.

125
00:09:52,286 --> 00:09:59,008
And it registered the config itself, in
its own Global Configs group, so the game

126
00:09:59,008 --> 00:10:01,000
can find it at start-up.

127
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:10,121
One rule to remember: after any change to
your states or scenes, press this button

128
00:10:10,121 --> 00:10:10,606
again.

129
00:10:10,606 --> 00:10:14,643
The config is generated, so never edit it
by hand.

130
00:10:17,143 --> 00:10:20,926
The game needs one scene that starts
everything.

131
00:10:20,926 --> 00:10:27,389
A new scene called Bootstrap, with a
camera and an event system for button
clicks,

132
00:10:27,389 --> 00:10:30,857
and an empty object for our start-up
script.

133
00:10:33,357 --> 00:10:35,243
Two lines start the UI.

134
00:10:35,243 --> 00:10:38,278
Initialize loads the config we built.

135
00:10:38,278 --> 00:10:40,820
Show State shows the main menu.

136
00:10:40,820 --> 00:10:46,643
That's it: you never put a UI manager in
your scene, it creates itself.

137
00:10:49,143 --> 00:10:50,007
Press Play.

138
00:10:50,007 --> 00:10:53,543
There's our menu, scaling in, with both
bars.

139
00:10:53,543 --> 00:10:58,571
Look at the Hierarchy: each element was
loaded as its own scene.

140
00:11:01,071 --> 00:11:02,802
Now the Store button.

141
00:11:02,802 --> 00:11:06,346
When it's clicked, we show the store
state.

142
00:11:06,346 --> 00:11:07,582
One line again.

143
00:11:07,582 --> 00:11:11,786
Drag the button into the field, and save
the scene.

144
00:11:14,286 --> 00:11:21,099
The nav bar does the same with two
buttons: Home shows the menu state, Store
shows

145
00:11:21,099 --> 00:11:22,429
the store state.

146
00:11:24,929 --> 00:11:26,937
Press Play and tap Store.

147
00:11:26,937 --> 00:11:33,446
The store slides in, and the bars stay
exactly where they are: they're shared, so

148
00:11:33,446 --> 00:11:34,893
they never reload.

149
00:11:34,893 --> 00:11:36,018
Home and back.

150
00:11:36,018 --> 00:11:41,643
Scenes load when a state needs them and
unload when nothing uses them.

151
00:11:44,143 --> 00:11:45,755
Last step: a popup.

152
00:11:45,755 --> 00:11:52,714
In this framework, a popup is just another
state, shown on top of the current one.

153
00:11:55,214 --> 00:11:56,404
A new element.

154
00:11:56,404 --> 00:12:03,375
The first thing under Root is a dimmed
backdrop that covers the whole screen,
then

155
00:12:03,375 --> 00:12:06,860
the panel with a message and two buttons.

156
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:14,001
It scales in, it fades out, and its
sorting order is a hundred, so it sits
above the

157
00:12:14,001 --> 00:12:15,786
screens and the bars.

158
00:12:18,286 --> 00:12:22,330
The parameters class carries the item's
name and price.

159
00:12:22,330 --> 00:12:24,977
Populate puts them into the message.

160
00:12:24,977 --> 00:12:30,565
And both buttons close the popup with Hide
UI: it removes the top state, and

161
00:12:30,565 --> 00:12:33,286
whatever was underneath simply stays.

162
00:12:35,786 --> 00:12:38,547
Its state has just this one scene.

163
00:12:38,547 --> 00:12:43,500
And because we changed the states, we
build the config again.

164
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:52,764
In the store, the Buy button fills in the
parameters and shows the popup state, with

165
00:12:52,764 --> 00:12:54,455
additive set to true.

166
00:12:54,455 --> 00:12:59,286
Additive means: put it on top, and keep
everything below it.

167
00:13:01,786 --> 00:13:04,824
Play, go to the store, and tap Buy.

168
00:13:04,824 --> 00:13:11,941
The popup opens on top with the item's
details, and the store stays right where
it

169
00:13:11,941 --> 00:13:12,288
was.

170
00:13:12,288 --> 00:13:15,500
Cancel, and it's gone, scene and all.

171
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:24,361
And that's Coin Shop: screens, shared bars
and a popup, every piece in its own

172
00:13:24,361 --> 00:13:29,335
scene, set up in the editor, with a
handful of lines of code.

173
00:13:29,335 --> 00:13:35,778
The showcase app in the repository goes
much further, so open it and see what's

174
00:13:35,778 --> 00:13:36,512
possible.

175
00:13:36,512 --> 00:13:38,143
Thanks for watching.
